# Excluded from the Comfy registry install bundle (dev-only weight).
# Syntax matches .gitignore; this layers ON TOP of git tracking --
# untracked files are already excluded by the publisher.
tests/
kibitz-runs/
.github/
.claude/

# 2026-08-22: throwaway probe/smoke scripts, never shipped node functionality.
# comfy-cli's security scan flagged exec() calls and semicolon-statements here
# (2.0.0-alpha.1 publish log) -- not user-facing, not documented in README, and
# not needed by anyone installing via the registry.
# 2026-08-28: THE WHOLE scripts/ TREE, and here is the reasoning.
#
# alpha.8 was the last version the registry marked Active. alpha.9, .10 and
# .11 are all Flagged. Between alpha.8 and alpha.9 three files were ADDED that
# shipped: two subprocess-spawning sweep scripts and a PowerShell .ps1. The
# tree also ships _otr_chatterbox_install.ps1, _otr_dia_install.ps1,
# _otr_indextts2_install.ps1, .bat and .cmd files -- INSTALLER scripts that
# spawn processes, inside a Python node pack. That is exactly the shape a
# security scanner is built to flag.
#
# NOTHING IN THE PACK IMPORTS scripts/. Verified: no `from scripts`/`import
# scripts` anywhere in nodes/ or __init__.py (the single mention in
# __init__.py is a comment). The node functionality is nodes/ plus
# __init__.py; scripts/ is dev harness -- headless runners, soaks, sweeps,
# probes, installers -- which a user installing from the registry never runs.
#
# 135 files stop shipping because of this line. That is the point: it removes
# the entire subprocess/PowerShell surface from the published bundle in one
# move, and it costs the installing user nothing.
#
# 2026-08-29, MEASURED: THE HYPOTHESIS ABOVE IS WRONG. KEEP THE LINE ANYWAY.
#
# alpha.12 shipped with this exclusion in force and was Flagged like the three
# before it. The two published bundles were then downloaded and compared file
# by file rather than reasoned about, and the result inverts the theory:
#
#   alpha.8   ACTIVE   814 files, 368 .py, 119 under scripts/, 28 .ps1/.bat/.cmd
#   alpha.12  FLAGGED  715 files, 277 .py,   0 under scripts/,  0 .ps1/.bat/.cmd
#
# The version carrying 28 PowerShell and batch INSTALLERS passed. The version
# carrying none of them failed. Worse for the theory, the executable trigger
# surface is IDENTICAL across the two bundles -- counting files that contain
# each pattern, outside scripts/ in both cases:
#
#   subprocess 33/33   Popen 9/9   base64 5/5   urlopen 2/2
#   pickle 1/1   __import__ 1/1   ctypes 2/2   marshal 1/1
#   shell=True 0/0   os.system 0/0   exec( 0/0
#
# The only two deltas are `eval(` 3->4 and `socket` 27->28, and BOTH new hits
# are COMMENT TEXT -- `.eval()` inside a prose note in otr_silent_composite.py
# and "(an input socket)" in otr_shot_lock.py. Not code.
#
# That also retires the two suspects this comment used to name: the google_*
# base64 decodes and the feed fetcher's urlopen calls are present in EQUAL
# NUMBER in the version the registry marked Active. They cannot be what
# distinguishes Flagged from Active. Do not re-open them.
#
# And the diff at the actual flag boundary -- alpha.8 -> alpha.9 -- adds no
# trigger pattern at all: six shipped .py files changed, zero new subprocess,
# exec, eval, base64, pickle, urlopen or socket call among them.
#
# WHAT REMAINS: nothing we shipped explains the flag, which points at a rule
# change on Comfy-Org's side between the two scans. Their scanner is a private
# repo and returns no reason, and there is no publisher self-service path to
# Active (CLAUDE.md 7A). Asking Comfy-Org is the move, and it is the
# operator's to make. Until then `latest_version` resolves to alpha.8, so
# every registry install receives 2026-08-25 code.
#
# THE EXCLUSION STAYS regardless, on its own merits: 132 tracked files of dev
# harness (119 of them were actually in the alpha.8 bundle, counted in the
# downloaded zip) -- headless runners, soaks, sweeps, probes, installers --
# that nothing in
# the pack imports and no installing user runs. Verified: no `from scripts` /
# `import scripts` anywhere in nodes/ or __init__.py. It is correct hygiene.
# It was simply never the fix, and the next session should not re-run this
# experiment expecting a different answer.
#
# 2026-09-02, RESOLVED (PBUG-20260902-04). The API now returns the reason:
# GET /nodes/comfyui-old-time-radio/versions?include_status_reason=true.
# Every flagged version carries 2 CRITICAL pylint findings -- "Prohibited
# string detected" for the session-bearer hidden input the writer's
# INPUT_TYPES declared (admin tags any-code-execute, credential-access) --
# plus ~156 INFO YARA findings (env reads, subprocess for ffmpeg, the opt-in
# cloud requests). That hidden input had been in the tree since 2026-06-01;
# alpha.8 shipped it and went Active, so the prohibition post-dates alpha.8's
# scan. Bundle CONTENTS were never the cause, which is exactly what the
# comparison above measured. The critical finding is fixed in code. The info
# findings are how every other pack on Comfy-Org/registry-backend's tracker
# reads too, and a version with ANY finding stays Flagged until an admin
# batch-approves it -- so after the next publish the move is a manual review
# request (docs/2026-09-02-registry-manual-review-request.md), not more diffs.
scripts/*

# 2026-09-01 (ship audit finding registry-flag-08): the scripts/ exclusion
# above silently broke three shipped TTS engines. eng_chatterbox.py (~line
# 76), eng_dia.py (~line 78) and eng_indextts2.py (~line 183), all under
# nodes/_otr_audio_engines/, each resolve their Path B subprocess worker as
# os.path.join(_REPO_ROOT, "scripts", "_otr_<engine>_worker.py") at runtime
# -- on a registry install those files never existed, so none of the three
# engines could ever start (RuntimeError: "worker script ... missing").
# Checked each worker for its own scripts/ dependencies: _otr_chatterbox_
# worker.py, _otr_dia_worker.py and _otr_indextts2_worker.py import only
# stdlib plus third-party packages (torch, torchaudio, chatterbox, dia,
# indextts, soundfile, numpy) resolved from the engine's own ISOLATED venv,
# never a sibling file under scripts/ -- no other scripts/ file needs to
# ship. The IndexTTS2 adapter is a special case. Its qualified voice-route
# runtime fingerprint intentionally hashes the whole adapter, so changing only
# its recovery wording would withdraw a human-audited route even though
# synthesis is byte-identical. Ship the exact installer its stable error names
# instead. The other installers and _otr_idx_download_weights.py remain
# GitHub-only; their adapters can name a URL without changing a qualified
# runtime receipt.
#
# scripts/ is rewritten to scripts/* (excludes the directory's CONTENTS,
# not the directory itself) because a gitignore-style negation cannot
# re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded. Verified against
# real git semantics in a scratch repo: with a bare "scripts/" line,
# `git check-ignore -v` still matched all three worker files against the
# "scripts/" rule even with the negations below present (never reached to
# decide); with "scripts/*", `git add -A` staged exactly the three worker
# files and nothing else under scripts/.
!scripts/_otr_chatterbox_worker.py
!scripts/_otr_dia_worker.py
!scripts/_otr_indextts2_worker.py
!scripts/_otr_indextts2_install.ps1
# 2026-09-05, and it is the SAME defect as the 2026-09-01 one above -- found by
# grepping the PUBLISHED alpha.20 zip rather than the tree. Exactly one runtime
# `scripts/` path in the whole pack was still unshipped:
# `nodes/_otr_video_engines/eng_mesh_stage.py:84` builds
# `STAGE_SCRIPT = os.path.join(_REPO_ROOT, "scripts", "otr_mesh_stage_blender.py")`
# and hands it to headless Blender, so on a registry install the mesh/3D lane
# could never start. The script runs INSIDE Blender's bundled python and
# contains no subprocess, exec, eval or network call (measured: zero hits).
# The check that finds this class is one line -- grep the shipped nodes for a
# join onto "scripts" and confirm each target is in the zip.
!scripts/otr_mesh_stage_blender.py
# The shipped IndexTTS2 installer calls this at its weights step, so excluding
# it left the one installer we DO ship pointing at a file that is not there.
# It is a huggingface_hub `snapshot_download` wrapper: no subprocess, no exec.
!scripts/_otr_idx_download_weights.py

